sometimes i think back in my life and  i'm amazed to remember the sheer number  of times i've  randomly come across a book while  looking for another book  and why is that  am i just incredibly lucky  is my mind making me believe that this  thing that i accidentally stumbled upon  is important  serendipity is hard to describe because  it sits somewhere between magic and  reality  faith and fact  luck and belief  today i've partnered with hp to try my  best to describe to you how a  series of serendipitous and seemingly  random encounters have led to my thesis  project  [Music]  i was going to call this video how i use  serendipity in my thesis project  but it was just wrong  i didn't really use serendipity serenity  happened to me there's a quote by john  barth from the book the last voyage of  somebody the sailor  you don't reach serendip by plotting a  course for it you have to set out in  good faith or elsewhere  and lose your bearings serendipitously  serendipity is built on happy accidents  but what makes serendipity unique  uh different from the million other  daily encounters that you forget about  is that the accident is meaningful to  you in some way and all we can do is  plant the seeds for those happy  accidents to occur  when i started my masters i had no idea  what i wanted to write my thesis about  but there was a reading that we were  given that really set out my philosophy  and approach towards my thesis and it  was an essay called a thousand plateaus  by duluz and guitare our western model  for knowledge and society is based on a  tree structure which have a seed a root  and branches and leaves that are all  traceable back to the origin a rhizome  is a plant structure that grows  horizontally and vertically in all  directions when rhizomes are separated  each piece can become a new plant a  rhizome doesn't have a central point or  a particular origin and for to losing  atari the rhizome is the model for the  post-modern world because it describes  research and thinking that allows for  multiple  non-hierarchical entry and exit points  the internet would be a great example of  this  it doesn't really have a point of origin  and  nothing can really control or shape it  or organize it its only rule is that it  grows you can take out any link or any  website without really damaging or  changing the internet it just exists  this kind of set the tone for our thesis  here just  general  confusion an architectural thesis is a  independent design research on a topic  selected by the student the student can  explore issues that he or she has  personal interests in and it's also an  opportunity to identify and elaborate on  a position within the field of  architecture  and for most people it's the final test  the culmination of their architectural  studies in the formal education system  some people have a pretty good idea what  they want to focus on but i started my  thesis here pretty clueless  and confused  and without  any vague idea of what direction i  wanted to go in  my masters was split into two years  the first was designed to explore all  the different avenues we could delve  into and exploring the different methods  of conducting research and methods of  communicating our ideas  and in the second year we narrowed down  on a topic  and we were kind of  swallowed  by this black hole called design  research and i'm gonna walk you through  the seven projects that i did in my  first year which all eventually led to  my thesis topic  so our very first assignment was all  about learning about thousands of  different perspectives in the  architectural discourse  and seeing which ones pulled our  interest and  starting to explore different methods of  representing our ideas  and this was the year of the chicago  biennial  [Music]  we were to pick three firms that were  participating and try to synthesize  their work and try to create some  connection between their work through a  medium of our choice this was in  preparation for our actual trip to  chicago where we would see the works in  person so for me  and for someone as indecisive as me  this felt like a monumental challenge  and i mean there's so many interesting  firms out there and like how do i know  what i'm interested in and what if i  pick the wrong firms does that mean i'm  gonna pick the wrong thesis topic  but  in the interest of deadlines and the  fear of failing  i forced myself to pick three that kind  of piqued my interest  and i'm not going to dwell on any  specific ideas or firms for the sake of  time of this video but feel free to  pause the video if you  want to read a more in-depth description  so the three firms were  liam young  studio detail  and i way  the project you could pick any medium  you wanted to and for my project i  decided to make a short video so that  week i opened up premiere for the very  first time i tried to learn the program  while at the same time trying to  make a project so after three days of  pure  frustration this is what i produced  [Music]  [Music]  when you think of the romantic image of  an architect you think of them drawing  day and night on their drafting board  coming up with these brilliant ideas but  in reality  a huge chunk of our time is actually  spent looking at books magazines and the  internet for inspiration  and that leads to a message from today's  sponsor hp a mouse is only a tool  but it is such an essential tool for the  modern day creative because it really  really is an extension of your hand  hp's new 930 creator wireless mouse is a  customizable mouse for architects or  creative professionals and it has seven  reprogrammable buttons that you can set  up shortcuts to and i've been really  loving this mail so far because it's  compatible with a lot of the softwares  that i use like sketchup photoshop and  of course chrome for when i'm scouring  through the internet for ideas it might  not seem like a lot but those few extra  seconds that i save with the 