 and engage  them in a new light and by doing this  it can kind of confront some of the  political and architectural ideas behind  something like the federal plaza  the projects of typological cannibalism  explicitly challenge the validity of  existing types to generate new urban  composites while renovations often aim  for respectful continuity with existing  architecture typological cannibalism  contrasts old and new to foreground the  need for typological revision  the next step was to establish your  convictions and represent them in the  form of architecture you could basically  do any kind of project that would  represent your convictions oh and you  have to make a comic strip  i was still pretty unsure of what i  wanted to do and what my conviction was  at this point but i didn't know that i  wanted to do something that challenged  the status quo in architecture the  status quo could be  represented by a typology a typology is  a building type that's become  replicated over and over and over again  to become recognizable by anyone across  different cultures  and here's my conviction  in order to create sustainable  lifestyles for the future we must not  only focus on creating efficient systems  for conservation and consumption but  also aim to actively engage users in the  understanding of urban  ecologies seeing the city as a  repository for cultural memory i  position ideas of sustainability through  the densification of urban areas and  creates new typologies amongst existing  structures the method of typological  cannibalism differs from adaptive reuse  as it aims to shock users into noticing  their new environments leading to  discomfort awareness and inspiration  this approach looks to deepen social  interactions while turning the elusive  idea of sustainability into tangible  realities  by the way i really hate how i was  writing at this time did i mention that  it's so freaking convoluted and  unnecessarily wordy and distanced and  impersonal  and nobody talks like this but  this is the methodology in which we  converse and construct systems of  knowledge in the intellectual world of  academia no  if i were to reword this in a way that  makes it accessible for everyone  it might go something like this  sustainability is not only about  creating physical systems for  conservation of energy  it is the process of engaging users to  understand and love their environment  the city is a complex organism full of  memory and culture and by working with  existing structures orders and  typologies  we can create new interactions  meanings and memories  typological cannibalism differs from  adaptive reuse and that it guides users  into noticing their new environments  leading to discomfort self-awareness and  awareness of his or her surroundings and  hopefully  inspiration  sustainability starts at the level of  the people  see how i'm making this a little bit  more personal and a little less didactic  i always try to use the pronoun we  i think it creates a sense of community  and  us  achieving this thing together also i  added the word hopefully  because this is something we can propose  but we can't really enforce  and i think it kind of leaves things  open-ended and open for conversation  and conversation's always good but  actually maybe the best way to explain  this concept is through my comic strip  [Music]  so how do you translate that into  architecture so this is the stage where  we possibly make the biggest leap from  idea to design

